1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,005
Turn these two phase controls.

2
00:00:03,045 --> 00:00:05,129
The light at the pupil stays equally bright,

3
00:00:05,169 --> 00:00:07,591
but the focal pattern moves and spreads.

4
00:00:07,661 --> 00:00:10,300
The aperture and total power have not changed.

5
00:00:10,370 --> 00:00:13,996
Which control should we change to make this pattern match the target?

6
00:00:14,866 --> 00:00:16,009
Predict the image forwards,

7
00:00:16,049 --> 00:00:18,946
then carry its error backwards to the design.

8
00:00:19,766 --> 00:00:22,849
Here is our one-dimensional pupil, the illuminated opening.

9
00:00:22,889 --> 00:00:26,969
We change only its phase, keeping the amplitude fixed.

10
00:00:27,039 --> 00:00:29,005
The phase contains a linear term for

11
00:00:29,045 --> 00:00:31,477
tilt and a quadratic term for defocus.

12
00:00:31,517 --> 00:00:34,330
Both coefficients are measured in radians.

13
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:37,451
Watch a phase ramp move the focal pattern sideways.

14
00:00:37,521 --> 00:00:40,132
Add quadratic phase at the same detector plane,

15
00:00:40,172 --> 00:00:44,001
and the pattern broadens. Interference redistributes the light.

16
00:00:44,071 --> 00:00:47,497
Multiply the fixed amplitude by a complex phase factor,

17
00:00:47,537 --> 00:00:51,163
rotating the field without changing its magnitude.

18
00:00:51,203 --> 00:00:53,413
A unitary Fourier transform propagates

19
00:00:53,453 --> 00:00:56,112
it while preserving total sampled power.

20
00:00:56,182 --> 00:00:58,353
The detector reads squared field magnitude.

21
00:00:58,393 --> 00:01:03,381
Across the full detector grid, these normalized intensities add to one.

22
00:01:04,201 --> 00:01:07,830
Choose the focused pattern with zero tilt and defocus as our target.

23
00:01:07,870 --> 00:01:12,819
Start with tilt and defocus coefficients of one point five and two.

24
00:01:12,889 --> 00:01:15,943
Subtract the target intensity at every detector sample.

25
00:01:15,983 --> 00:01:18,612
These differences are the residuals,

26
00:01:18,652 --> 00:01:21,129
including the pattern's faint outer structure.

27
00:01:21,198 --> 00:01:23,075
Square and sum the residuals,

28
00:01:23,115 --> 00:01:25,781
then divide by twice the target's squared norm.

29
00:01:25,821 --> 00:01:28,839
This dimensionless loss measures the mismatch.

30
00:01:28,909 --> 00:01:31,197
Keep that target and normalization fixed.

31
00:01:31,237 --> 00:01:34,680
Zero loss means matching this sampled intensity,

32
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:37,744
not necessarily recovering a unique phase.

33
00:01:38,564 --> 00:01:41,680
Now ask how a tiny parameter change affects the loss.

34
00:01:41,720 --> 00:01:45,540
Follow the same calculation backwards using the chain rule.

35
00:01:45,610 --> 00:01:48,763
A positive residual marks a sample that is too bright.

36
00:01:48,803 --> 00:01:52,077
A negative residual marks one that is too dim.

37
00:01:52,147 --> 00:01:55,088
Divide each residual by the target's squared norm,

38
00:01:55,128 --> 00:01:57,394
and multiply by the focal field.

39
00:01:57,434 --> 00:02:00,397
This starts the reverse intensity calculation.

40
00:02:00,467 --> 00:02:03,568
Apply the conjugate transpose of the Fourier transform.

41
00:02:03,608 --> 00:02:07,872
This gathers every detector sample's influence back at the pupil.

42
00:02:07,942 --> 00:02:10,888
This backward field represents mathematical sensitivity.

43
00:02:10,928 --> 00:02:15,119
No physical light travels backwards in this calculation.

44
00:02:15,189 --> 00:02:18,993
At the pupil, a small phase change rotates the complex field.

45
00:02:19,033 --> 00:02:23,091
That converts field sensitivity into real phase sensitivity.

46
00:02:23,161 --> 00:02:25,651
Weight this sensitivity by position for tilt,

47
00:02:25,691 --> 00:02:27,743
and squared position for defocus.

48
00:02:27,783 --> 00:02:30,851
Sum across the pupil to obtain both derivatives,

49
00:02:30,891 --> 00:02:33,376
the components of our design gradient.

50
00:02:34,196 --> 00:02:36,154
Check those derivatives before using them.

51
00:02:36,194 --> 00:02:40,871
Nudge one coefficient forwards and backwards by the same small amount.

52
00:02:40,941 --> 00:02:44,505
Recompute both losses and divide their difference by the separation.

53
00:02:44,545 --> 00:02:49,023
This central finite difference estimates the same local slope.

54
00:02:49,093 --> 00:02:53,390
Try several perturbation sizes. Large nudges miss the local slope;

55
00:02:53,430 --> 00:02:56,903
extremely small ones expose floating-point rounding.

56
00:02:56,973 --> 00:03:00,442
Here both parameter derivatives agree over a useful range.

57
00:03:00,482 --> 00:03:03,878
They are evaluated at the identical starting design.

58
00:03:04,748 --> 00:03:06,395
That checks the implemented derivative.

59
00:03:06,435 --> 00:03:11,309
It does not establish that the optical model captures a real device.

60
00:03:12,129 --> 00:03:14,964
Move opposite the gradient to seek a lower loss.

61
00:03:15,004 --> 00:03:17,475
The step length controls how far we move.

62
00:03:17,545 --> 00:03:21,477
Surprisingly, the first step increases defocus while reducing the loss.

63
00:03:21,517 --> 00:03:25,878
The coefficients act together; shrinking each one is not the rule.

64
00:03:25,948 --> 00:03:29,965
Recompute the loss after each trial. Shorten an unsuccessful step,

65
00:03:30,005 --> 00:03:32,757
and accept it only when the loss decreases.

66
00:03:32,827 --> 00:03:35,865
Watch forty updates bring this pattern close to the target,

67
00:03:35,905 --> 00:03:38,537
with the same aperture and power throughout.

68
00:03:39,407 --> 00:03:43,528
Start both coefficients at three instead. The loss stalls above zero,

69
00:03:43,568 --> 00:03:46,180
although we know a zero-loss design exists.

70
00:03:46,250 --> 00:03:48,386
A correct gradient gives local information,

71
00:03:48,426 --> 00:03:51,011
not a guarantee of the best design.

72
00:03:51,831 --> 00:03:55,305
Differentiable optics means an optical model has usable

73
00:03:55,345 --> 00:03:59,121
derivatives with respect to its chosen design variables.

74
00:03:59,191 --> 00:04:01,452
Automatic differentiation applies derivative

75
00:04:01,492 --> 00:04:03,463
rules through a computation graph.

76
00:04:03,503 --> 00:04:07,473
Here, we wrote the reverse calculation explicitly.

77
00:04:07,543 --> 00:04:09,685
Adjoint methods efficiently carry a

78
00:04:09,725 --> 00:04:12,263
scalar objective's sensitivity backwards.

79
00:04:12,303 --> 00:04:14,915
An automatic-differentiation system can

80
00:04:14,955 --> 00:04:17,338
include an optical solver's adjoint.

81
00:04:17,408 --> 00:04:19,849
For curved lenses, the chain must include

82
00:04:19,889 --> 00:04:22,285
ray intersections and Snell refraction.

83
00:04:22,325 --> 00:04:24,229
Away from grazing incidence,

84
00:04:24,269 --> 00:04:27,374
local derivatives follow one intersection branch.

85
00:04:27,444 --> 00:04:31,512
Clipping or switching ray paths can break a smooth local derivative.

86
00:04:31,582 --> 00:04:33,651
For joint optical and digital design,

87
00:04:33,691 --> 00:04:35,677
place the loss after reconstruction.

88
00:04:35,717 --> 00:04:40,511
Differentiate through both stages to update both sets of parameters.

89
00:04:40,581 --> 00:04:42,355
These are extensions of the chain.

90
00:04:42,395 --> 00:04:47,196
Our computed example optimizes only a phase element's intensity pattern.

91
00:04:48,016 --> 00:04:49,743
Predict fields and measurements forwards.

92
00:04:49,783 --> 00:04:52,536
Carry a precisely defined loss backwards

93
00:04:52,576 --> 00:04:54,644
to the parameters you can change.

94
00:04:54,714 --> 00:04:58,079
Check the derivative, take a step, and measure the loss again.

95
00:04:58,149 --> 00:05:00,014
This model uses one wavelength and

96
00:05:00,054 --> 00:05:02,976
ignores polarization. Test what it omits:

97
00:05:03,016 --> 00:05:05,356
sensor noise, fabrication limits,

98
00:05:05,396 --> 00:05:08,269
and performance beyond the optimized samples.

99
00:05:08,339 --> 00:05:11,657
Differentiable optics connects image error to design changes.

100
00:05:11,697 --> 00:05:15,313
A useful gradient still needs a useful model.

